# Belt and braces alongside netlify.toml.
#
# netlify.toml's redirects apply when Netlify BUILDS the site; this file is
# copied into dist/ by Vite and so travels with a direct folder deploy too.
# Without one of the two, /book/<slug> and /booking/<id> 404 — and those are
# the URLs that arrive cold from a text message or a confirmation email,
# where a 404 is a lost booking rather than a refresh annoyance.
# Studio S1 — the site renderer's own entry, above the catch-all (first match wins).
/site/*    /site.html   200
# Studio: the detailer's preview link, detailingplatform.com/p/<link>
/p/*       /site.html   200
# Obvious guesses a visitor or search engine tries (GitHub #35).
/login    /app   301
/signup    /app   301
/sign-in    /app   301
/sign-up    /app   301
/signin    /app   301
/faq    /#faq   301
/pricing    /index.html   200
/terms    /index.html   200
/privacy    /index.html   200
/reset    /index.html   200
/book/*    /index.html   200
/booking/*    /index.html   200
/plan/*    /index.html   200
/unsubscribe/*    /index.html   200
/admin    /index.html   200
/preview    /index.html   200
/invite/*    /index.html   200
/job/*    /index.html   200
/website    /index.html   200
/website/preview    /index.html   200
/settings/payments/connected    /index.html   200
/app    /index.html   200
/app/today    /index.html   200
/app/calendar    /index.html   200
/app/money    /index.html   200
/app/clients    /index.html   200
/app/business    /index.html   200
# Anything else is a REAL 404: the app shell still loads, so its router draws
# the existing 'That page does not exist' screen, but the status is 404 and
# search engines drop the address. A new route in app/src/main.jsx must be
# listed above or it will 404 (tests/route-contract.test.mjs checks this).
/*    /index.html   404
